"comparison": "Both C and BAC define VIEs based on ASC Topic 810 criteria regarding insufficient equity or lack of controlling equity interests. C identifies a VIE if equity is insufficient for financing without subordinated support or if investors lack decision-making power/proportional risk absorption. BAC defines a VIE similarly as an entity that 'lacks equity investors or whose equity investors do not have a controlling financial interest.' While C explicitly details the two specific criteria (insufficient equity or lack of power/absorption), BAC summarizes this as lacking a 'controlling financial interest through their equity investments.' Both firms focus on the sufficiency and control aspects of equity to determine VIE status.",
